WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
- They knew that this bill
didn't ban books, okay?

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.305 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause we had already
discussed that

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:06.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
the day before in
the rules committee,

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:09.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
before the debate came
to the whole House.

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:11.277
They knew it didn't ban books,

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they went down there
and said it anyways,

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.115
because they knew a lot
of their constituents

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:17.050 align:start size:88% position:13%
wouldn't do the due
diligence to find that.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.818
- Yeah, and stated a
little bit differently,

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:21.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
the bill does
nothing to do that.

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:23.723
What it does do is
empower parents, and
they come in and go,

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:26.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well, you're just gonna
empower this mob to go after."

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:27.794
Well, hold on a second.

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:29.029
There are books

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.997
that I don't want to be
in the school library.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.699
Let me be perfectly clear.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.634
The question is which library,

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.202
elementary, high school?

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.304
And then parents should be
able to have the say there.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.440
That's literally
all this bill did,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.142 align:start size:84% position:16%
was say parents should
be able to know.

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:45.111
(curious pop music)

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Dave Rubin, Thomas
Massie, Chip Roy.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.186
How was that for an intro?

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:54.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Sounds good to me.
- Nailed it.

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:55.555
- Solid, this is it.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.457
This is the end of
my little DC tour.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:59.092
I'm finishing up with you guys.

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:00.994
Something about
this giant table.

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:03.930 align:start position:0%
I feel like this could end
both of your careers right now.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.231
- You saved the best for last.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:07.500
- You guys are on
trial right now.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.902
All right, Thomas, I
gotta start with you,

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:10.203
because everyone's
gonna be wondering

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.072
about that thing right there.

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:15.542 align:start size:84% position:16%
You have the debt
clock on your lapel.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:16.543
- He bought me one, I'm
still waiting on it.

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.678
- [Dave] Yeah, where's yours?

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:22.148
- You know, some people
have a pacemaker up here,

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:23.716
a lot of these old senators.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.218
I've got an anxiety maker.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:27.887 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm trying to make
their hearts skip beats,

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.722
and become concerned

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.725
by looking at the debt
increasing each day.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.261
I wrote the software in here.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.896
I made the case.

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.132 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've got some 3D printed
ones I'm working on

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:40.967
so Chip Roy can have a copy.

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.036 align:start size:97% position:3%
But it goes to the treasury's
website once a day,

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:44.971 align:start size:81% position:19%
gets the actual
debt to the penny,

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.140
and then makes its best guess

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
based on the average debt
per second over the last year

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.812
about what the debt is
at this very moment.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:55.381
- I'm gonna guess that, when
you're back home in Kentucky,

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:57.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
or, I should say Kentucky
and Texas, of course,

00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:58.651
when you're back
home in Kentucky,

00:01:58.651 --> 00:01:59.919
people probably dig it.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:00.854
You come here and
everyone's like,

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.088
"Could you put that thing away?"

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.623
Little bit of that?

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.459
- The Democrats, I had one
in an elevator look at it

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.495
and say, "Is that
a doomsday clock?"

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:10.563
And I said, "Yes, ma'am, it is."

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:14.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, they're always
escalating that situation.

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:16.636
So you guys are on the
freedom side of things,

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:17.837
I would say.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.339 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're on what I
would generally call

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
the more libertarian side,
freedom side of things.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:23.309
I sense, after three days here,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.411
that that really
is the direction

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:28.081
that the Republican
party is going in.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:32.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you agree with that or
am I just being pollyannish?

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.122
- I mean, I'll say that
I think that, certainly,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.991
there is more of an
appetite for that

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.093
among a larger block of
the Republican party.

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:43.062
That doesn't necessarily mean
that's the overall direction.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:45.732
We still have a lot of people
we've gotta get there, right?

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:47.367
There is still an
enormous dominance

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.602
of what I would call the
war caucus in this town,

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:52.238
and I say that as someone
who, child of the eighties,

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.073
and very much believe in
a very strong military.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.042
Use sparingly but forcefully.

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:58.645 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we've got a kind of
stranglehold on this place

00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:00.146
of the way we've
always done things,

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.916 align:start position:0%
and part of the issue here with
freedom is reminding people

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:05.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
why we have a limited
government of diffused powers

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:06.319
and why that's important

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:08.087 align:start size:84% position:16%
for preserving and
protecting our rights.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:10.523
Nothing illuminated
that more than COVID.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:13.793 align:start position:0%
Nothing illuminated the
dangers with centralizing power

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:17.230
and giving power to a handful
of cronies like Anthony Fauci

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.464
than what we just witnessed.

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:20.333
Thomas has been one of
the leaders on that,

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:21.601
along with Rand Paul.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:22.969
I've tried to help, and others,

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we've got to
continue to elevate that.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:28.074 align:start size:97% position:3%
There is an increased
appetite for it, but, in part,

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:29.108 align:start size:84% position:16%
because these
people in Washington

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.543
respond to their constituents,

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:31.744
there's a lot of Americans

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:34.013
who want us to preserve
and protect liberty.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.315
- Let's be honest.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:36.816
Let's be honest, though.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:40.320
The fight that happened
the first week of January

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.923 align:start position:0%
over who would be the next
speaker was very consequential.

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:46.726
And a lot of people say,
"Oh, that looked so bad.

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.494 align:start size:84% position:16%
The Republican party
was in disarray."

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:51.231 align:start size:84% position:16%
No, that is how it's
supposed to work.

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:54.400
We're not supposed to have
all these backroom deals

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.002
and everything's baked.

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:59.305
And what came out of
that is, actually,

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:02.942
Chip Roy and I ended up
on the rules committee,

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.577
along with Ralph Norman.

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:08.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, conservatives, the
people who are most likely

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.783
to advocate for freedom
through legislation,

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.285
have a blocking position

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
on every bill that
comes to the floor now.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:19.192
And also, for the first time
since I've been in Congress,

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:22.695
we're actually imposing
time to read the bills.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
Here we are, three
months into this session,

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.668
and every bill that's
come to the floor,

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.870
you've had 72 hours to read.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, 72 hours, I know
people back home are like,

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:36.209 align:start size:94% position:6%
"There's not time to
read a bill in 72 hours."

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:38.411
- But it's better
than 72 minutes.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.713
- Yeah, 72 minutes was the norm.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:41.948
- Right, right.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.483
- And the other
thing is 72 hours

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.985
gives people back home time
to read about it in the news

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.554
and then call the
other congressman.

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.023
So it's a heads up.

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:51.257
You know, you could
give our colleagues

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:52.492
a year to read a bill

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:54.160
and some of them are
not gonna read it,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
or they could read
it, know what's in it,

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:57.830
know it's bad, and
still vote for it.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.033
What's important is
the American people

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.934
have a chance in the news cycle

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.037
to find out what's
going on up here,

00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:05.838 align:start size:88% position:13%
call their congressman,
and influence it.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.775
- So to that point, I sat
down with Speaker McCarthy

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:09.776
at the Capitol yesterday,

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:11.511
and I sensed, actually, now,

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:13.579
maybe it wasn't when you
guys were in the fight,

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.316 align:start size:88% position:13%
that he kind of is
happy with the results.

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:17.717
I think he does feel like

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.820 align:start position:0%
maybe it empowered him a
little bit by you guys pushing.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.021
Do you think that's fair?

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:23.289
- Look, I think that's fair.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:24.824
I mean, of course he's
happy with the results

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:26.225
'cause he got to be speaker.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.094
- He did get the gig.

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:29.329
It was a messy couple days.

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.331
- But in fairness
to Kevin, right,

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:32.665
I mean yesterday
he sent a letter

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.200
to the President of
the United States

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.735 align:start size:81% position:19%
detailing some
spending restraint

00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:38.071 align:start size:84% position:16%
that a lot of us have
been pushing for.

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:39.605
He did uphold his
end of the bargain

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.708 align:start size:97% position:3%
in ensuring that several of
us got on the rules committee,

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:43.076
that we have more conservatives

00:05:43.076 --> 00:05:45.345 align:start position:0%
on the appropriations
committee and other committees,

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.513
that we've been adhering
to a number of the rules

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:49.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we had asked
for back in December.

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:50.283
So all of that's good,

00:05:50.283 --> 00:05:51.918
and I think, when that happens,

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.487
there's a unity that
builds around that.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:57.523
Now, getting to 218
on anything is hard,

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:59.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you gotta only lose
four, five, six guys or gals

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:02.862
and it's difficult, but, look,

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:05.665
I think what we saw
coming out of January

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:07.367
was a renewed spirit that,

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:10.536
when some people stand
up on what they believe,

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:13.339
you can prove that the
sky's not gonna fall.

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:17.009
You can have 435 people in
the chamber debating stuff

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:18.578
and actually move the needle,

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:20.413
rather than just kind of
retreating to your corners

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:22.014 align:start size:88% position:13%
doing press conferences
and walking away.

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:23.216
And I think we showed that,

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:24.283
and I think Kevin's the
beneficiary of that.

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:26.018
- Yeah, let me give an example

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.855 align:start size:97% position:3%
of how the new system
actually works out for Kevin.

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:30.656
So, to get the majority,

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:32.225
he went out and did
a lot of campaigning,

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:33.726
and one of his signature issues

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:37.897 align:start position:0%
that a lot of people care about
is reclaiming our schools,

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:41.367
basically letting parents know
what their kids are learning.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:44.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so, there was a bill
that was coming to the floor,

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:49.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
HR5, that would impose
a requirement on
every school system

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:52.945
in the United States that
you publish your curriculum

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:55.481
and the list of the books
that are in your library,

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:57.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
so parents-
- Real Fascist stuff,

00:06:57.984 --> 00:06:58.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you listen to AOC,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:02.088
- Well, believe it or
not, Chip Roy and I

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.257
had a small issue
with that bill,

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.793
which a lot of members
of our conference did.

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:08.294
We were like, well, you know,

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:11.197
we prefer the Department of
Education doesn't even exist.

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:16.335
So the question, like, if by
reforming it or directing it,

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:19.505
are we violating our
principle of federalism

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:21.874
and leaving things
up to the states?

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:23.810
And so, they asked me,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Is there any way you
could vote for this bill?"

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:27.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I said, "Yeah, if we
have an amendment on the bill

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:30.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
that eliminates the
Department of Education."

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:33.953 align:start size:88% position:13%
If I can show a
preference legislatively

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.388
through my votes on the floor,

00:07:35.388 --> 00:07:37.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
not just say something
on a website,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.193
that my preference is to
get rid of this department

00:07:40.193 --> 00:07:43.496 align:start size:97% position:3%
rather than reform it, I
could vote for the final bill,

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:44.764
regardless of how it turns out.

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:48.000
Well, and Chip was the same way.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
He said-
- I was able to offer

00:07:49.235 --> 00:07:50.403
some amendments that
would require dollars

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:51.838 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be block granted
to the states,

00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:53.339
which would be sort
of the next step.

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:55.341 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, I favor abolishing it,
but if you can't do that,

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:56.843
can we at least just
give it to the states

00:07:56.843 --> 00:07:57.777
and let them decide?

00:07:57.777 --> 00:07:59.011
And if you can't do that,

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:00.079
can we at least let the dollars

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.314
eventually get to the parents

00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:02.815 align:start size:81% position:19%
to be able to just
use themselves?

00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:04.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we got a vote on all
three of those amendments.

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:07.220
- And what surprised me is Kevin

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.223
voted to remove all the power

00:08:10.223 --> 00:08:12.859 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the Federal
Department of Education,

00:08:12.859 --> 00:08:16.762
to micromanage elementary
and secondary education.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, for the first time
since Jimmy Carter

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.133 align:start size:84% position:16%
set up this Department
of Education

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:23.936
as a reelection
ploy that failed,

00:08:23.936 --> 00:08:26.472
we had a referendum in the
House of Representatives

00:08:26.472 --> 00:08:28.508 align:start size:84% position:16%
on whether they
should exist or not

00:08:28.508 --> 00:08:29.909
in our schools back home.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:31.277
And Kevin joined us on saying,

00:08:31.277 --> 00:08:32.278
"No, they shouldn't.

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:33.880
Give it back to the schools."

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.282
- So this might sound
sort of amateur hour,

00:08:36.282 --> 00:08:39.185 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the department, obviously,
is still going to exist,

00:08:39.185 --> 00:08:40.953
but it just won't
have authorization

00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:43.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
when it comes to
these types of issues.

00:08:43.623 --> 00:08:46.359
- And let me be clear, before
everybody gets real excited.

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:47.827
Our amendments didn't pass.

00:08:47.827 --> 00:08:49.095
- Right.

00:08:49.095 --> 00:08:50.897
- But we still voted
for the final bill,

00:08:50.897 --> 00:08:54.433
because we got a chance to
put everybody on record,

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:55.768 align:start size:81% position:19%
and now people
back home can say,

00:08:55.768 --> 00:08:57.937 align:start size:81% position:19%
"Would I rather
have a congressman

00:08:57.937 --> 00:09:00.139 align:start size:84% position:16%
that wants to take
the federal government

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:01.974
out of my kids' education,

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:03.476
or a congressman who wants
the federal government?"

00:09:03.476 --> 00:09:05.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, and one
final point on that,

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:06.445
to your point, so
what did we pass?

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:08.047
We passed legislation,
as Thomas described,

00:09:08.047 --> 00:09:09.248
that empowers parents

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:10.750 align:start size:84% position:16%
to know what their
kids are being taught.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.518
We put out in the record a
whole bunch of garbage books

00:09:12.518 --> 00:09:15.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
with all sorts of total
smut and ridiculous things

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:17.056 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were in libraries
and in curriculum,

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:18.424
and we were demonstrating that.

00:09:18.424 --> 00:09:19.959
- That's the underlying bill.

00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:21.460
- And that's the
underlying bill.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.996 align:start size:97% position:3%
But for me to get comfortable
with federal power

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:26.132
being used to go tell a
local school district,

00:09:26.132 --> 00:09:28.834
you're gonna require parents
to be able to see this stuff.

00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:31.137 align:start size:97% position:3%
I needed to demonstrate
that my personal preference

00:09:31.137 --> 00:09:33.105 align:start size:84% position:16%
is to get the feds out
of it all together.

00:09:33.105 --> 00:09:35.374
But in the absence
of that, then, yeah,

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:37.176
a parent ought to have a right

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:40.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
to at least know how these
dollars are being used

00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:41.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what's being
taught to their kids.

00:09:41.681 --> 00:09:44.083
And so, I think getting
that series of votes,

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:45.551
restoring the way things work,

00:09:45.551 --> 00:09:46.919 align:start size:81% position:19%
which is a part
of that agreement,

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:49.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
empowered Kevin and empowered
us to come together.

00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:51.290
- Yeah, just to cap all that,

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.059
that bill probably
would've failed.

00:09:53.059 --> 00:09:54.293
- [Dave] Correct.

00:09:54.293 --> 00:09:56.295
- And Kevin, one of
his major initiatives

00:09:56.295 --> 00:09:57.530
that he campaigned on

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.365
wouldn't have been able
to pass in a majority.

00:09:59.365 --> 00:10:00.299
- If we don't pick the rules.

00:10:00.299 --> 00:10:02.268
- If we didn't have,

00:10:02.268 --> 00:10:06.005
if we didn't help him
put into the process

00:10:06.005 --> 00:10:09.508
an ability for conservatives
to express their preferences.

00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:11.310 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So was the calculation
by you guys that,

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
yes, you would prefer that
the federal government

00:10:13.212 --> 00:10:16.282
not do anything, but not
every state is going to do

00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:19.218 align:start position:0%
what perhaps Texas and
Kentucky and certainly Florida,

00:10:19.218 --> 00:10:21.988 align:start size:97% position:3%
Iowa, Arkansas, a couple other
states are doing right now?

00:10:21.988 --> 00:10:24.690 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it was sort of your only
way, in essence, right?

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause Cali is never gonna
do this sort of thing,

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:28.094
New York, et cetera.

00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:29.629 align:start position:0%
- Well, I mean, again, and going
back to preferences, right?

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:31.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would rather,
even though I think

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.666
what California does is insane,

00:10:33.666 --> 00:10:35.501 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would rather just get
the feds out of it and say,

00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:37.236
"If you wanna be in insane
in California, go ahead.

00:10:37.236 --> 00:10:38.704
But don't mess with
my kids in Texas

00:10:38.704 --> 00:10:40.506 align:start size:84% position:16%
or don't mess with
the kids in Kentucky."

00:10:40.506 --> 00:10:42.375
But in the absence of that,

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:44.176
if we're going to,
as we do currently,

00:10:44.176 --> 00:10:46.078
have federal dollars flow,

00:10:46.078 --> 00:10:48.714 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think it is within reason
of me being able to say

00:10:48.714 --> 00:10:50.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would prefer
this other outcome,

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:51.751
to be able to go
in and say that,

00:10:51.751 --> 00:10:53.185
"Yeah, parents have a right."

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:55.087
And, frankly, even in
Texas and Kentucky,

00:10:55.087 --> 00:10:57.256 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's not like every
parent in Texas

00:10:57.256 --> 00:10:58.891
knows exactly what's going on.

00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:00.459
So where those federal
dollars are flowing.

00:11:00.459 --> 00:11:02.294 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, they'll have a
little bit more ability

00:11:02.294 --> 00:11:03.562
to push for that.

00:11:03.562 --> 00:11:05.698
- So, when you guys
see AOC afterwards,

00:11:05.698 --> 00:11:08.934
talk about how this bill was
basically passed by fascists

00:11:08.934 --> 00:11:11.504 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it's like, man, she
needs some better education,

00:11:11.504 --> 00:11:13.139
'cause she doesn't
know what it means.

00:11:13.139 --> 00:11:15.207
Or Hakeem Jeffries, you know,

00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:16.709 align:start size:88% position:13%
they don't want to teach
about the Holocaust.

00:11:16.709 --> 00:11:19.578 align:start position:0%
I mean, these were the responses
you got out of these guys.

00:11:19.578 --> 00:11:21.480 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've asked this to every
Republican I've sat down with

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:22.748
for the last three days,

00:11:22.748 --> 00:11:24.617
are you talking to these
guys in the back rooms,

00:11:24.617 --> 00:11:26.485
going, "What the hell
are you talking about?

00:11:26.485 --> 00:11:28.921
You know that's not legit."

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:31.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I mean, in the rules
committee, we debated this,

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:33.492
and Chip was right to point out,

00:11:33.492 --> 00:11:36.929
they knew that this bill
didn't ban books, okay?

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:38.731 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause we had already
discussed that

00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:40.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
the day before in
the rules committee,

00:11:40.232 --> 00:11:42.835 align:start size:84% position:16%
before the debate came
to the whole House.

00:11:42.835 --> 00:11:44.737
They knew it didn't ban books,

00:11:44.737 --> 00:11:46.605 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they went down there
and said it anyways

00:11:46.605 --> 00:11:48.574
because they knew a lot
of their constituents

00:11:48.574 --> 00:11:50.476 align:start size:88% position:13%
wouldn't do the due
diligence to find that.

00:11:50.476 --> 00:11:52.111
- Yeah, and stated a
little bit differently,

00:11:52.111 --> 00:11:54.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
the bill does
nothing to do that.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.149 align:start position:0%
What it does do is empower
parents and they come in and go,

00:11:57.149 --> 00:11:59.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well you're just gonna
empower this mob to go after."

00:11:59.852 --> 00:12:01.153
Well, hold on a second.

00:12:01.153 --> 00:12:02.388
There are books

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:04.356
that I don't want to be
in the school library.

00:12:04.356 --> 00:12:06.025
Let me be perfectly clear.

00:12:06.025 --> 00:12:07.960
The question is which library,

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:09.528
elementary, high school?

00:12:09.528 --> 00:12:11.630
And then parents should be
able to have the say there.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.766
That's literally
all this bill did,

00:12:13.766 --> 00:12:15.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
was say parents should
be able to know.

00:12:15.067 --> 00:12:17.403
- Did you guys see on TV,

00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:18.904
was it about a week ago?

00:12:18.904 --> 00:12:20.573
DeSantis is going through the
books that they've removed.

00:12:20.573 --> 00:12:24.076
It's basically two books that
were pretty much pornographic,

00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:26.278 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the media, the mainstream
media, had to cut away

00:12:26.278 --> 00:12:28.614 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they can't show
pornography on ABC.

00:12:28.614 --> 00:12:29.548
- That's funny.

00:12:29.548 --> 00:12:30.916
- I pulled up one of those books

00:12:30.916 --> 00:12:32.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the committee
that I was sitting on

00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:34.453
where we had some witnesses,

00:12:34.453 --> 00:12:36.889 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I was starting to
show, and I had to stop.

00:12:36.889 --> 00:12:39.425
I showed the cover,
and then I opened it.

00:12:39.425 --> 00:12:40.693
I was like, "I can't show this."

00:12:40.693 --> 00:12:42.528 align:start size:84% position:16%
I literally couldn't
put it on CSPAN.

00:12:42.528 --> 00:12:44.663 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's the kind of thing
the government's saying.

00:12:44.663 --> 00:12:45.798
- But it can be third grade.

00:12:45.798 --> 00:12:47.233
- But they wanted to say

00:12:47.233 --> 00:12:49.602 align:start position:0%
that they were removing Clemente
and Rosa Parks, all false.

00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:52.772
Those books were
left on the shelves

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:54.974
or they were reviewed
as part of a process

00:12:54.974 --> 00:12:56.909 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then put back on the
shelves, and that's the truth.

00:12:56.909 --> 00:12:59.745
- By the way, they don't
read the bills we vote on.

00:12:59.745 --> 00:13:01.080
I've read a few of their bills.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.982
I've read The Green New Deal,

00:13:02.982 --> 00:13:05.084
because it's only 14 pages.

00:13:05.084 --> 00:13:06.252
And when I got done with it,

00:13:06.252 --> 00:13:08.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
I realized why
it's only 14 pages.

00:13:08.254 --> 00:13:11.357 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's only 64 crayons
in that big box.

00:13:11.357 --> 00:13:12.725 align:start size:72% position:28%
- That's good.
(all laugh)

00:13:12.725 --> 00:13:14.426
I saw it comment,
I saw it coming.

00:13:15.594 --> 00:13:17.997
Is the difference
between Republicans

00:13:17.997 --> 00:13:21.767
and Democrats right now sort
of as wide as you've ever seen

00:13:21.767 --> 00:13:24.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
in your how many
years now in Congress?

00:13:24.036 --> 00:13:25.871
- 10 1/2 for me.

00:13:25.871 --> 00:13:27.373
How many for you?

00:13:27.373 --> 00:13:30.176
- Yeah, I'm in my third
term, so four and change.

00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:33.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So is that difference
wider than ever right now?

00:13:33.612 --> 00:13:35.047
- It's gotten wider.

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:39.752 align:start position:0%
There were some moderate
Democrats when I first got here.

00:13:39.752 --> 00:13:42.354
They kind of just finally
all got washed out here.

00:13:43.522 --> 00:13:45.925
Through attrition,
they were going down.

00:13:45.925 --> 00:13:50.429
But I don't blame AOC
for her positions.

00:13:50.429 --> 00:13:51.764
I really don't.

00:13:51.764 --> 00:13:54.533
I blame the people
that voted for her.

00:13:54.533 --> 00:13:57.369
What you see here,
the division you see,

00:13:57.369 --> 00:14:00.239
it truly is, and people may
not like when I say this,

00:14:00.239 --> 00:14:03.275 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a reflection of the
American voters' preferences.

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:06.145
And I think the country's
become more polarized,

00:14:06.145 --> 00:14:08.347
and that's why you see
Congress more polarized.

00:14:08.347 --> 00:14:11.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, if you
look at, right now,

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:12.985
there are places
in the northeast

00:14:12.985 --> 00:14:14.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
or in certain sectors
of this country

00:14:14.587 --> 00:14:15.888
where the people
are still masked.

00:14:15.888 --> 00:14:19.358
I mean, you flip on the
NBC control room, right?

00:14:19.358 --> 00:14:22.027
The control room, they're
all sitting there in masks.

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:23.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
Good grief, man,
like four years ago.

00:14:23.562 --> 00:14:25.097 align:start size:81% position:19%
We weren't wearing
masks in Texas.

00:14:25.097 --> 00:14:27.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
I went out to Lakey,
Texas, which I represent,

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.869
which is about two and a
half hours from my house,

00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:32.872
and went out to, they
were doing goat roping

00:14:32.872 --> 00:14:36.242
and it was a Labor
Day weekend in 2020.

00:14:36.242 --> 00:14:39.612
Of course, the northeast
elites and all that stuff,

00:14:39.612 --> 00:14:41.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, they were
cowering in their corners

00:14:41.046 --> 00:14:42.548
and hiding and masked up.

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:43.816 align:start size:81% position:19%
These guys were
all out, you know,

00:14:43.816 --> 00:14:45.885
the kids and everybody
were out in the rodeo

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:48.020
and they're roping goats,

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.456 align:start position:0%
and we were all getting together
and having food and dinner.

00:14:50.456 --> 00:14:51.824
It was totally different worlds,

00:14:51.824 --> 00:14:53.726
two different worlds, truly.

00:14:53.726 --> 00:14:55.527
And that still exists today,

00:14:55.527 --> 00:14:57.029
that divide, that difference,

00:14:57.029 --> 00:14:59.231
that different view about
how you do these things,

00:14:59.231 --> 00:15:00.966
and I think that is reflected

00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:03.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the representatives
in Washington.

00:15:03.335 --> 00:15:07.072 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Which is why we need more
solutions that are state-based

00:15:07.072 --> 00:15:08.908 align:start size:84% position:16%
rather than federal.
- Correct.

00:15:08.908 --> 00:15:10.542
You have to be able to
agree to disagree, right?

00:15:10.542 --> 00:15:12.678
I mean, the reason I
believe in federalism

00:15:12.678 --> 00:15:16.382
and the reason I hedged a bet
on the parents' rights bill

00:15:16.382 --> 00:15:19.251 align:start position:0%
and wanted to go, well,
because all things being equal,

00:15:19.251 --> 00:15:21.020
the only way this
republic survives

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:23.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
is if we can literally
agree to disagree

00:15:23.222 --> 00:15:24.623
under our federal system.

00:15:24.623 --> 00:15:26.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
If we're gonna tell
each other what to do,

00:15:26.625 --> 00:15:28.127
it's going to break us apart,

00:15:28.127 --> 00:15:29.628
and that's what we've gotta
figure out how to resolve.

00:15:29.628 --> 00:15:31.263
- Is that really the fight
more than anything else?

00:15:31.263 --> 00:15:32.998
Because it seems to me
that the red states,

00:15:32.998 --> 00:15:35.968
and you guys know, I lived in
Cali and now I'm in Florida,

00:15:35.968 --> 00:15:37.670
I feel like I live in
a different country.

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.205
But it seems to me
that the blue states

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:42.408 align:start size:84% position:16%
will never stop coming
for the red states.

00:15:42.408 --> 00:15:44.543
The red states, I think,
could, for the most part,

00:15:44.543 --> 00:15:46.912 align:start position:0%
be like, "Hey, we're just gonna
take care of our business,

00:15:46.912 --> 00:15:48.414
and that's it."

00:15:48.414 --> 00:15:50.549 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the blue states are not
gonna leave well enough alone,

00:15:50.549 --> 00:15:54.420
and I sense that's what
is a big oncoming fight.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.788
What do you think?

00:15:55.788 --> 00:15:57.289 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I mean, I
certainly think that's been

00:15:57.289 --> 00:15:58.390
the direction things have
been going for a while.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.259
I mean, certainly,

00:16:00.259 --> 00:16:02.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether it's the kind
of DEI type stuff

00:16:02.461 --> 00:16:04.396 align:start size:97% position:3%
or whether it's, for example,
climate change, right?

00:16:04.396 --> 00:16:05.931
They're gonna say, well, no,

00:16:05.931 --> 00:16:07.433
you don't have a right to
be over there doing that,

00:16:07.433 --> 00:16:09.201
and they're gonna try to
shut down our way of life

00:16:09.201 --> 00:16:12.571
and kind of export, frankly,
their misery to the rest of us.

00:16:13.472 --> 00:16:14.773
- I left that misery.

00:16:14.773 --> 00:16:15.607
I don't want that imported.

00:16:15.607 --> 00:16:17.042
- And, you know, look,

00:16:17.042 --> 00:16:18.777 align:start size:84% position:16%
one thing I do
believe in, though,

00:16:18.777 --> 00:16:20.245 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that there's an ebb
and flow to things.

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:21.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
I do believe in
the power of ideas.

00:16:21.647 --> 00:16:23.182
I believe in the
power of freedom.

00:16:23.182 --> 00:16:24.483
I believe in the power of
the fundamental principles

00:16:24.483 --> 00:16:26.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
upon which this
country has founded,

00:16:26.118 --> 00:16:28.487
and that leaders of this
country have an obligation

00:16:28.487 --> 00:16:31.323
to project confidence
in those principles

00:16:31.323 --> 00:16:32.891
and go fight for them.

00:16:32.891 --> 00:16:35.027
You know, I was talking
to a group this morning,

00:16:35.027 --> 00:16:37.896
and all these folks that
get June 6th and D-Day

00:16:37.896 --> 00:16:40.099
and they talk about their dads
or granddads and their family

00:16:40.099 --> 00:16:41.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were storming the
beaches of Normandy

00:16:41.467 --> 00:16:42.868
to a wall of bullets.

00:16:42.868 --> 00:16:44.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they talk about Washington
crossing the Delaware

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:45.904 align:start size:81% position:19%
and Texas, the
guys on the Alamo.

00:16:45.904 --> 00:16:47.139
And you know, I say,

00:16:47.139 --> 00:16:48.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Oh, you're talking
about all that pride."

00:16:48.073 --> 00:16:49.508
And then they go,

00:16:49.508 --> 00:16:51.276 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Man, you were real courageous
in that speaker's fight,

00:16:51.276 --> 00:16:52.778
'cause they were tweeting
mean things about you."

00:16:52.778 --> 00:16:55.014
It's like, look, come on guys.

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:56.315
You wanna fight for freedom?

00:16:56.315 --> 00:16:58.117
Just get out there and
say what you believe.

00:16:58.117 --> 00:17:00.219 align:start position:0%
Stand up like I did at a
Chamber of Commerce event today

00:17:00.219 --> 00:17:01.587
and say DEI is garbage.

00:17:01.587 --> 00:17:03.088
Stop promoting it.

00:17:03.088 --> 00:17:05.391
It's hurting our recruiting
at the Department of Defense.

00:17:05.391 --> 00:17:06.692
